---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Toc from "../components/Toc.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";

const sections = [
  { href: "#lands", label: "Lands" },
  { href: "#nations", label: "Nations" },
  { href: "#vassals", label: "Vassals" },
];
---

<Base
  title="Claims · Meridian"
  description="A guide to claiming land on Meridian: land banks, upkeep, tiers, nations, and vassals."
  path="/lands"
  analytics
>
<main class="doc">
  <h1>Claims</h1>
  <p class="lead">A guide to claiming land on Meridian. It covers how you claim, how land banks and upkeep work, how lands unlock tiers, how they combine into nations, and what happens to a nation that loses a war of vassalage.</p>

  <Toc sections={sections} />

  <h2 id="lands"><span class="num">1</span>Lands</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Run <code>/lands create &lt;name&gt;</code> to found your land. The first chunk is free; every chunk after that costs a flat <strong>5 G</strong>.</li>
    <li>You can own only <strong>one land at a time</strong>. If you don't own a land, you may be trusted in <strong>one</strong> other land. If you do own one, you cannot be trusted in any other &mdash; ownership and trust don't stack.</li>
    <li>When naming your land you can use a hex code to set its color on the world map, for example <code>&lt;#FF5555&gt;ExampleLand</code>. Minecraft only recognizes a specific set of hex colors.</li>
    <li>Your land has its own shared bank, separate from any player's inventory. Claim costs and upkeep are paid from this bank, and any trusted member can deposit into it. <strong>There is no personal bank to fall back on</strong> &mdash; gold has to actually be sitting in the land bank.</li>
    <li>Every land pays weekly upkeep on its claimed chunks, at <strong>2 G per chunk</strong>.</li>
    <li>Larger lands cost more to maintain. Before you claim aggressively, make sure your land has the gold to support it.</li>
    <li>You can rent or sell sub-areas of your land to other players without trusting them.</li>
    <li>Lands unlock tiers purely by how much gold is sitting in the land bank &mdash; trusted-member count no longer matters. Once a tier is earned, it's never lost, even if the balance later drops.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="tier-wrap">
    <table class="tier-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Ruler</th><th>Land bank</th><th>Grants</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Manor</td><td>Lord</td><td class="num-col">&ndash;</td><td>Starting tier &mdash; 7 chunks</td></tr>
        <tr><td>County</td><td>Count</td><td class="num-col">50 G</td><td>+5 chunks (12 total)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Duchy</td><td>Duke</td><td class="num-col">100 G</td><td>+13 chunks (20 total)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Grand Duchy</td><td>Grand Duke</td><td class="num-col">400 G</td><td>+23 chunks (30 total)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="tier-note">The gold has to be sitting in the land bank, not in anyone's pocket or a chest. Tiers stack: a Grand Duchy carries every grant below it. Upkeep stops applying to the server entirely once your land joins a nation, because the land then pays tax to the nation instead.</p>

  <h2 id="nations"><span class="num">2</span>Nations</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Only a land that has reached <strong>Grand Duchy</strong> can found a nation, for <strong>100 G</strong>.</li>
    <li>When a land joins a nation it stops paying upkeep to the server and instead pays tax directly to the nation. The nation's ruler sets that rate.</li>
    <li>In turn, the nation owes its own upkeep to the server, at <strong>1 G per chunk</strong> across the combined total of its lands.</li>
    <li>Relations between nations are publicly tracked in-game.</li>
    <li>Nations advance through their own tiers based purely on how many lands they have &mdash; no gold or member-count requirement.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="tier-wrap">
    <table class="tier-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Ruler</th><th>Lands (excl. capital)</th><th>Grants</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Principality</td><td>Prince</td><td class="num-col">&ndash;</td><td>Starting tier</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Kingdom</td><td>King</td><td class="num-col">3</td><td>+20 chunks per land, +1 land slot</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Empire</td><td>Emperor</td><td class="num-col">5</td><td>+35 chunks per land, +3 land slots</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="tier-note">The capital doesn't count toward the lands requirement &mdash; a Kingdom needs 3 other member lands beyond it, an Empire needs 5. A nation is a commitment to a tax base that can support its members, not a title bought once.</p>

  <h2 id="vassals"><span class="num">3</span>Vassals</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>A nation that loses a war under the Vassalage goal is absorbed by the winning nation as a restricted member.</li>
    <li>Vassals pay taxes to their overlord that can run higher than normal nation taxes.</li>
    <li>The only way to become independent again is to declare and win a war of independence against your overlord. See <a href="/war#consequences">Wars &rarr; Consequences</a>.</li>
  </ol>

  <Callout>Wars are the only time claimed land can be entered or captured by an enemy. Read the <a href="/war">Wars</a> document before you get into a conflict.</Callout>
</main>
</Base>
